 ('Stakhanovite', 'Darya Ivanovna Udarnikova', 'Collect 250 at Poluchka instead of 200.', "You beat your quota by 1,400%. Everyone else's quota has been raised accordingly. They'd like a word.", 'Named for miner Alexei Stakhanov, who in 1935 reportedly dug 14 times his norm, with a lot of quiet help.', 'Udarnik: a "shock worker," the official title for quota-smashers.'),
 ('Red Director', 'Pyotr Semyonovich Zheleznov', 'Workshops and Kombinats cost you 50 less, to a minimum of 30.', 'You know nothing about steel, but your class background is impeccable.', 'Red directors were loyal Party men promoted to run factories, often over the trained engineers.', "Zhelezo: iron, the one thing he's sure his factory makes."),
 ('Kolkhoznik', 'Matryona Filippovna Repkina', 'Collect only 150 at Poluchka, but pay no fees on Brown or Light Blue spaces.', "You feed the nation. The nation will let you know if there's any left.", "Kolkhoznik: a collective-farm peasant. From 1932 they were denied internal passports and couldn't legally leave the farm.", 'Repka: "little turnip," after the folk tale where the whole village pulls together on one vegetable.'),
 ('Spekulant', 'Arkady Lvovich Dostavalov', 'Once per Year, take an appointment at half price. Anyone may denounce you for free.', 'You know a guy who knows a guy. None of you have ever met.', 'Spekulant: a black marketeer. Reselling goods for profit was a criminal offense, and a thriving one.', 'Dostat: "to obtain." Soviet shoppers didn\'t buy scarce goods, they obtained them.'),
 ('Seksot', 'Zinaida Pavlovna Ushkina', 'You may denounce twice per round instead of once.', "You're a wonderful listener. Everyone says so, mostly in your reports.", 'Seksot: short for "secret collaborator," an informant for the NKVD, the secret police.', 'Ushko: "little ear."'),
 ('Old Bolshevik', 'Nikifor Savvich Pervov', 'Take 2 Blat with this card, but every Suspicion gain is doubled.', 'You stormed the Winter Palace alongside several men who, it turns out, never existed.', 'Old Bolsheviks joined the Party before 1917. They were prestigious, well connected and the chief targets of the Great Purge.', 'Pervy: "first," as in first to join the Party and first on the list.'),
 ('Spets', 'Vladimir Karlovich Vintikov', 'You may build unevenly across a colour group, but gain +1 Suspicion whenever anyone draws a Sabotage card.', "The only person who knows how the turbine works, and so the prime suspect when it doesn't.", 'Spets: a "bourgeois specialist" trained before the Revolution. They were routinely blamed for "wrecking" when plans failed.', 'Vintik: "little screw." Stalin once toasted ordinary citizens as the little screws of the state machine.'),
 ('Poet', 'Vsevolod Aristarkhovich Rifmachov', 'Draw two cards and choose one. Your Suspicion is set to at least 2 when you take this card.', 'Your ode to the tractor was well received. Your earlier work is being reread.', 'From 1934 all writers belonged to one state union. Stalin called them "engineers of human souls."', 'Rifmach: a rhymester, a hack who writes verse to order.'),
 ('Red Army Officer', 'Fyodor Trofimovich Shashkin', 'Pay no railway fees and take 1 Blat with this card, but gain +1 Suspicion whenever any citizen is disappeared.', 'Decorated hero of the Civil War. Your old commander has just confessed to something on your behalf.', 'The 1937-38 purge gutted the officer corps, including three of the five marshals.', 'Shashka: the cavalry sabre of the Civil War.'),
 ('Apparatchik', 'Boris Borisovich Bumazhkin', 'Your Inspection bribes are halved.', 'You stamp forms. Which forms? That information requires a form.', 'Apparatchik: a full-time functionary of the Party machine, the "apparat."', 'Bumazhka: a scrap of official paper. As the saying goes, "without a bumazhka you\'re a bug."'),
 ('Prodavets', 'Klavdia Petrovna Podprilavkina', 'On Ochered, take a Blat token without skipping a turn.', 'The shelves are empty. Under the counter, however, lies a land of plenty.', 'Prodavets: a shop clerk. Controlling scarce goods made it a position of real power.', 'Iz-pod prilavka: "from under the counter," where the real inventory lived.'),
